Here's the full picture:

---

**D15 Condo | $2.4M | $6,800/month rental**

| Metric | Annual ($) | Yield |
|---|---|---|
| Gross rental income | $81,600 | **3.40%** |
| Less: Maintenance (MCST @ 1.5%) | ($36,000) | (1.50%) |
| Less: Property tax (NOO rates) | ($13,248) | (0.55%) |
| Less: Insurance | ($600) | (0.03%) |
| Less: Vacancy buffer (1 month) | ($6,800) | (0.28%) |
| **Net rental income** | **$24,952** | **1.04%** |

**Gross yield: 3.40% | Net yield: 1.04%**

---

**Key flags to raise with the client:**

1. **Maintenance figure is the main wildcard.** At 1.5% of purchase price ($36K/year = $3,000/month), this is well above typical D15 MCST rates ($400–$600/month). If actual MCST is $500/month ($6K/year), net yield recovers to ~**2.29%**. Confirm the actual maintenance schedule before using this number.

2. **Leveraged = cash-flow negative.** At 75% LTV (~$1.8M loan, 3.5–3.8% SORA, 25yr), monthly mortgage repayment is ~$9,000–$9,400 — exceeding the $6,800 rental. The property bleeds cash every month unless bought cash.

3. **NOO property tax is non-negotiable.** $13,248/year at the IRAS progressive rates on a $81,600 AV. First-time landlords consistently underestimate this.

4. **Rental income tax.** Net rent is assessable under personal income tax — material at higher brackets (up to 24% marginal rate in SG).

**Bottom line:** 3.4% gross is in line with D15 market norms. Net yield at 1.04% is thin for a $2.4M outlay — the 1.5% maintenance assumption is doing most of the damage. Verify the actual MCST first; the real net yield is likely 2.2–2.5%.


